\f3\fs26 The Commissioner cut Shakti off and immediately made another call. \'93He fucked it up even more than we could have hoped. He took one look, called it suicide and then went back to bed. There was no evidence collected. We can\'92t be accused of a cover-up if there is nothing to cover.\'94\
\'93Good, Mr. Commissioner,\'94 said the CEO. \'93The blank finished the job, but lost a Gyges disc and then disappeared. He is probably dead, but he must be located. My agents are surgical instruments and they work alone. We don\'92t have the investigative capacity to find him\'94 The CEO lied. He already had an agent on the case.\
 \
\'93Consider it done.\'94\
\'93Discreetly\'94\
\'93Always.\'94 Assured the Top Cop of the PTD.\
He thought finding this dead blank should not be too difficult, it wasn\'92t the first time one had fallen off the grid. Finding a live blank was usually the difficult bit. They were invariably terminally ill and could drop dead at any second, but this made them very useful for assassinations. Even to company specialists, murder was a particularly thorny affair. Killing was no problem, of course. It is easy to pop someone off, but getting away with it was the hard part, especially when the killing was a part of a series directed at a specific political or, more usually, commercial end. How did you sever obvious links between the victims? How do you keep yourself safely out of the glare of an investigation? Termocorp enlisted the terminally ill with the promise of ongoing financial assistance for their families. All they had to do was kill and then die. If they were caught, they were expected to suicide or remain silent until death; about half an hour of interrogation would usually do it. Only one blank had ever been caught alive. Luckily, it was a woman who had children to protect. A man would have cracked, but she never uttered a sound, foolishly believing Termocorp would actually take care of her family.\
Once in a while, when things went pear shaped, the Commissioner would be called upon to iron out some problems. Completely untrustworthy, he was a model public servant. Piloting safe through the pitfalls and moral ambiguities of a career in law enforcement required sheer genius and possession of highly malleable ethics. Throughout his career he had carefully and sometimes intuitively, selected the correct asses to lick and right dicks to suck. Naturally, the owners of the correct asses and the right dicks were, without exception, corrupt to the core. Once they had their hooks in, they were dug in deep, never to be removed. The Commissioner owed more than a few favours. \
He called upon his most competent and discreet Investigator, Detective Superintendent Hayley Lanning to head the search for the missing blank, Emil Kalheri. Lanning, a Doctor of Laws, possessed a brilliant analytical mind and unsurpassed knowledge of matters legal, easily matching any attorney. Her capacity to cut through the irrelevant and identify salient issues was matchless among her untalented peers, but her most peculiar attribute was her integrity \'96 she had some, in fact she was the most prominent example of an exception proving the rule. She was unlike any other public servant in all of the Dominion in that she had the innate ability to combine honesty and tact, skills previously unknown in the public sector. She had her fair share of enemies within (and without) the ranks of the Police, but even her most ardent critics could not help but acknowledge her superior mind and envy her integrity, all longing to know what a clear conscience felt like.\
\'93You want me to find a missing person?\'94 quizzed an unbelieving senior detective. \'93This sounds more like a job for Shakti.\'94\
\'93Definitely not Shakti.\'94 The Commissioner instantaneously shot back, faintly smiling. \'93I need your special skills. It is a very sensitive matter and you are the best I have.\'94\
\'93No need to piss in my pocket, sir. I am always happy to help.\'94 
\f4\i This is just insulting. 
\f3\i0 She didn\'92t add. Her internal bullshit-meter was madly flapping red flags and she needed to know what the hell was going on. \'93Is there anymore background on this gentleman?\'94\
\'93I\'92ll transfer the file to you, but there isn\'92t much. He\'92s disappeared and I need him located.\'94\
\'93Why is a level one labourer attracting this much attention? Why is he that important? He\'92s a nothing, in fact less than a nothing, but I can\'92t draw up a strategy without all the background.\'94 She saw his smile had suddenly been replaced with a blank look and she decided she should push him no further. \'93You know, if you fail to plan you plan to fail.\'94\
\'93Clich\'e9s from you? I never thought you would be so commonplace.\'94 The Commissioner laughed, trying to deflect her question. It might have been better to give this one to Shakti, he silently considered, at least he doesn\'92t ask questions. \'93I know this is a little below your dignity, but you can keep things under wraps and that is what is needed here.\'94\
\'93I\'92ll be on my own then?\'94\
\'93Unfortunately, yes. You will have the resources of this office, but that is all.\'94 He said austerely.\
\'93I will have to dump a few other cases on my team, but I will get on it straight away. The Nightclub Vampire case will have to be put on hold. I am the only one who has a handle on that one.\'94 \
\'93You\'92ll be working out of this building. I\'92ll arrange an office for you. That will be all, Superintendent.\'94\
